<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albanians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Albanians are a people of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> who speak the Albanian language. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> many converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and served in the army and administration. In the empire's last decades, Albanian national movements and revolts contributed to the loss of Ottoman territory in the Balkans, and Albania declared independence in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albanians.jpg" alt="Albanians" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerd 72 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_traditional_male_folk_group_from_Skrapar.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Albanians speak Albanian, an Indo-European language and the only surviving representative of the Albanoid branch of the Paleo-Balkan group. Most scholars hold that they descend at least partly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>, but which other Paleo-Balkan groups contributed to their ethnogenesis is still debated. The name Albanoi first appears in the 2nd century CE in Ptolemy, who describes an Illyrian tribe around present-day central Albania. The first certain reference to Albanians as an ethnic group is by the 11th-century chronicler Michael Attaleiates, who places them in the theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a>.</p><p>The Komani-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Kruja</a> culture, attested from late antiquity to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> in central and northern Albania, is significant for the study of the transition from the classical population to the medieval Albanians. Their own name for themselves, Shqiptarë, is the most common native ethnonym, while Arnaut and Arbanasi are among the names used by neighbours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in Albania was under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until the 8th century, when its dioceses passed to the patriarchate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. After the schism of 1054 the north became identified with Roman Catholicism and the south with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a>. In 1190 Albanians established the Principality of Arbanon, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kruje">Krujë</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> overpowered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the 15th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>, a union of Albanian principalities led by Gjergj Kastrioti Skanderbeg, resisted successfully.</p><p>By the 17th and 18th centuries many Albanians had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, which offered equal opportunities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. More than 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Viziers</a> were of Albanian origin. The Albanian wālī Muhammad Ali founded a dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> until the middle of the 20th century. Albanians declared independence in 1912 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a>, and the borders set after the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> left about half the ethnic Albanian population outside them. Albania was governed by a communist government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> from after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> until 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Albanian society was traditionally organised in tribes, the fiset, each a kin community usually tracing descent through the male line to one forebear. Tribal life depended on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanun">Kanun</a>, a code of oral customary law, and had chiefs, councils and blood feuds. It remained largely intact in the north until the communists took power in 1944, and is regarded as the only European tribal system of this kind to survive to the mid-20th century.</p><p>Albanians are traditionally both Christians and Muslims: Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a> and Bektashis. Albanian communities also settled outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, such as the Arbëreshë in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the Arvanites in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Around five million Albanians live across the Balkan Peninsula, and a large diaspora lives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and beyond.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albanians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The geographer Strabo, writing around the turn of the era, described the <b>Albanians</b> as a union of 26 tribes, each with its own language, who had once had separate kings but were now ruled by one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1992 census counted 10,394,026 inhabitants: Serbs 62.6%, <b>Albanians</b> 16.5% and Montenegrins 5%, with Hungarians, Yugoslavs, Muslims, Romani, Croats, Bulgarians and others.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the newly gained areas an estimated 49,000–130,000 <b>Albanians</b> were expelled, settling mainly in Kosovo, events that marked the beginning of the Serbian-Albanian conflict; from 1830 to 1876 up to 150,000 Albanians had already emigrated or been expelled from the principality.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Kosovo <b>Albanians</b> demanded a republic from the protests of 1981, and Serb opposition to the provinces' autonomy grew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Gottfried Schramm speculated that the <b>Albanians</b> descend from the Thracian Bessi, but there is no archaeological or linguistic support for this.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Despite the name, members included many <b>Albanians</b>, Arabs, Armenians, Circassians, Greeks, Kurds and Jews.</blockquote><a href="/movement/young-turks">Young Turks · People</a></li></ul></section>
